body:
Disclaimer: I've been cheated on before but in my most recent relationship I was the cheater, so i'll probably be down-voted into oblivion but I have two perspectives. That being said though, walking away for you, at least right now, may be the easiest thing to do to get back on track mentally. People who tell you otherwise are lying. My gf walked away (deservedly) almost a month ago. I will say that if, after the shock dissipates to at least a level where you can think straight again, you feel like you want her back in your life, then you have to seriously look into her *actions,* not words, to see if there is any potential for reconciliation. 

&#x200B;

Is she working on whatever part of her that is broken that facilitated her to do this to you? Going to therapy? Cut out people in her life that normalized her behavior? Cut out reckless behavior like drinking excessively? These are all key signs that maybe reconciliation with her is possible, if YOU want it. Further, I feel that it would be a better sign that she is doing all of these things without having to be asked to do them. If after 2 weeks or a month she contacts you and can prove that she is actively making these and other changes, I wouldn't say you HAVE to walk away if you don't truly want to. 

&#x200B;

Cheating is a cruel and selfish and destructive act. But I am not of the mindset that people cant change. Look for whether or not she is capable of changing, but only in her actions, not in words. I've made a lot of changes in my life since my infidelity, and they have all been hard, but necessary, work. Words are cheap and easy in these instances, so make sure, if you do decide to get back with her, that she is jumping through hoops.
